<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Chamba State</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Chamba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Himalayan hill kingdom in the Ravi valley ruled by one dynasty for some 1,400 years, known for its stone temples, copper-plate grants and Chamba rumal embroidery.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that the state was founded around 550 by Maru, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> of the Varman line, with its first capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharmour">Brahmapura</a> (modern Bharmour) in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> valley. In the 10th century Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahil-varman">Sahil Varman</a> moved the capital down the valley to Chamba, named, by tradition, after his daughter Champavati.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chamba is unusually well documented for a hill state, thanks to many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate land grants and inscriptions. Its Rajas built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi-narayan-temple-chamba">Lakshmi Narayan</a> temple complex and patronised Pahari <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a> and the embroidered Chamba rumal. Remote and mountainous, it accepted Mughal overlordship, paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> and kept most internal affairs in its own hands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> in 1846 the British confirmed the Rajas in their state. Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuri-singh">Bhuri Singh</a> (1904–1919) founded the museum that bears his name. Chamba merged into the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> in April 1948 and is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chamba-state.jpg" alt="Chamba State" width="200" height="134"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Nataraja at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drapeau_Chamba.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chamba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sahil Varman, Bhuri Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chambeali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chamba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1249&amp;focus=chamba-state" aria-label="Chamba State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6634 994 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chamba State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6954 1250L7364 1601M6954 1250L7364 1602M6954 1250L7018 1386M6954 1250L6940 1236"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0M6940 1236h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6954 1250h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6954 1250h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chamba. <a href="/atlas?year=1249&amp;focus=chamba-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
